#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:5
#: b7af6844882342d6b7cf5d0aef4894cc
msgid "Fixed Wing FAQ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:7
#: 19b413e090b549e19bb0e6df0ea97bc4
msgid "This is a set of frequently asked questions with answers. It is updated when we see questions on `discuss.ardupilot.org <https://discuss.ardupilot.org/c/arduplane>`__ that are not sufficiently answered in the rest of the docs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:10
#: d0250c0d29304a2e8dee957e8726232c
msgid "When reading this FAQ please refer to the :doc:`full parameter list <parameters>` for an explanation of each parameter which is mentioned in the answers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:13
#: c8297e10326b486a921b9151767df943
msgid "How do you stop a Nitro plane from cutting the engine in flight?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:15
#: 478c4f5d75bb423f8ebfd333e2b14641
msgid "For internal combustion motor planes (which can be prone to cutting the engine at low throttle), you should use the following settings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:17
#: ac2eac88da414c47a592010831c448db
msgid ":ref:`THR_MIN<THR_MIN>` =10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:18
#: 53f74aae36774e1fac1b49410a3b0d18
msgid ":ref:`THR_PASS_STAB<THR_PASS_STAB>` =1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:19
#: fcae1cab155944cb83ddd73daf9d4c40
msgid ":ref:`THR_SUPP_MAN<THR_SUPP_MAN>` =1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:21
#: 2c89b65e239d4853833e32bde4f959df
msgid "That will prevent the throttle dropping below 10%, but will give you manual throttle control for idling while on the ground, and manual throttle control in stabilisation modes (such as FBWA and STABILIZE) for shutting down the motor when you need to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:26
#: 019e36244a37417e9598e9321be19025
msgid ":ref:`THR_SLEWRATE<THR_SLEWRATE>` can also aid in prevention of a nitro engine stalling in flight by slowing the throttle advance from going wide open to quickly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:30
#: f5312559e9564656a616534d9a1ec34c
msgid "How do you inhibit gyro calibration?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:32
#: 9423fd925a9b44089e3469a1a2768b55
msgid "Set the parameter :ref:`INS_GYR_CAL<INS_GYR_CAL>` =0. That will skip the gyro calibration done on each startup. The last gyro calibration from when you calibrated the accelerometers will be used instead. This also means you don't need to hold the plane still when booting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:38
#: b89fd3ee2cf848b2b6b06834ae93a3aa
msgid "The downside of skipping gyro calibration is that your gyros may have drifted since you last calibrated them, perhaps due to temperature changes. If you have :ref:`ARMING_REQUIRE<ARMING_REQUIRE>` enabled then the arming checks will catch that problem if it is very large, but in general we recommend not skipping gyro calibration unless you have a good reason to use it. This is especially the case if you have the EKF enabled, as it is particularly sensitive to gyro error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:47
#: 2a71d62ff19c47bea7b7e6f37d771844
msgid "Tuning is too difficult, how do I make it easier?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:49
#: 43080845aa094d2989b41d96e75cf0b6
msgid "Please see the :ref:`documentation for autotune <automatic-tuning-with-autotune>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:52
#: a9496f8672654583a4968f967310a3dc
msgid "How do I setup reverse throttle on a IC plane?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:54
#: 1ce03e7be175422985e27359240018fb
msgid "Some planes (mostly nitro or petrol planes) have a reversed throttle servo, so lower PWM values on the throttle channel gives more throttle not less."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:58
#: 440f6694b6fa49f39ab0a735af23e0c0
msgid "After you setup reverse throttle make sure you test correct failsafe by turning off your transmitter while on the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:61
#: 49cec290478f4e3fa0c7e74a01927a9f
msgid "The servo library allows you to reverse the throttle output channel without affecting your RC inputs or failsafe configuration. to do this, set :ref:`SERVO3_REVERSED<SERVO3_REVERSED>` to 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:66
#: df9978c18c3740b1bc0c77f5f89be200
msgid "What happens if an airspeed sensor fails in flight?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:68
#: ade26195452f4073bc5ef9fde3c29cd4
msgid "What happens when airspeed fails depends on the type of failure. The most likely scenarios are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:71
#: e98ef562c49f40119734e8761df477c6
msgid "the I2C cable fails with a digital airspeed sensor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:72
#: a9293bb67c884c48acb0a06edee529b0
msgid "the pitot tube gets partially blocked, leading to incorrect scaling of the pressure (either too low or high)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:74
#: dd5d896261754a13b8282b0c15cf6bf3
msgid "the pitot tube gets completely blocked, leading to fixed readings (either too low or high)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:77
#: 830b303a954d4a3c8a8522231d7fe07b
msgid "In the first case where an I2C failure is detected the code will detect the failure and stop using the airspeed sensor. The aircraft will continue to fly with the algorithms it uses for an aircraft with no airspeed sensor. The ground station will be notified of the failure, and should display a warning to the user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:83
#: 376ecf6eecce4ec094c48441765ea695
msgid "For the other two cases the code does not currently have a reliable way to detect an airspeed failure. If the failure leads to a low airspeed reading then if the plane is in an auto-throttle mode (such as AUTO, GUIDED, LOITER or RTL) then the plane will tend to lose altitude as it tries to gain speed. The amount of altitude it will lose depends on how low the airspeed reading is. If the airspeed reading is low enough then it may trigger a fast enough descent to crash the aircraft."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:91
#: f24ff71999914dec8341058187157b55
msgid "If the failure leads to a too high airspeed reading then the plane will slow down to try to keep its airspeed at the target airspeed. If the reading is high enough then the plane may slow down enough to cause a stall and crash the aircraft."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:96
#: d871ce4788f24dc999815904257d7367
msgid "We are looking at ways of detecting partial airspeed sensor failures and hope to add some protection into the code in the future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:100
#: 644980125f3e4dc0a35fddcb2f51a474
msgid "Why don't my surfaces move enough when using flaperons, elevons, or v-tail?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:102
#: d40d7b1384fd4907a3b094f00d0ea079
msgid "You are probably using the default :ref:`MIXING_GAIN<MIXING_GAIN>` of 0.5. The default is setup to prevent channel saturation. If you instead want to be able to have full deflection then try setting :ref:`MIXING_GAIN<MIXING_GAIN>` =1.0 or something in between."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:108
#: e3f7a8f692f54cb4b7312766b4505e51
msgid "How do I get a good flare in automatic landing?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:110
#: 7e7256d862994a9e8e5ef499d4dbd4a2
msgid "Please see :ref:`this page <automatic-landing>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:113
#: 04675bc2f7be4c87adc545372367cb41
msgid "How do I reset all parameters to defaults?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:115
#: 778e87a0758d4fbdad27ef1cd70c687b
msgid "To reset all parameters set the parameter :ref:`FORMAT_VERSION<FORMAT_VERSION>` to 0 and reboot. When ArduPilot starts up it checks if :ref:`FORMAT_VERSION<FORMAT_VERSION>` has the correct value, and if it doesn't it wipes the parameters, which resets them to the default values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:121
#: e08e864976aa4d4b97a018de9a8c5e96
msgid "What does \"Bad AHRS\" mean on a ground station?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:123
#: 4fe7f7d601a845d1862d19f080f9bd17
msgid "It means the \"Attitude Heading Reference System\" is unhealthy. That is the software that determines the attitude of the aircraft. Possible causes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:126
#: a80f9c9995604a928a0ea9abd828bd3a
msgid "Accelerometer calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:127
#: 100373906ee342ccbc745f16563f732c
msgid "GPS has not acquired a good enough lock (#sats, HDOP, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:128
#: aee92ceec0ab4920bdd550b3040c7911
msgid "EKF has not settled (tilt/yaw initialization, origin not set yet, variances, etc.). **\"EKF IMUx using GPS\"** message will be displayed on ground control station when EKF is ready."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:131
#: 6a00a5e1670f4b478773a49c92617456
msgid "if no compass is enabled, the **\"EKF IMUx using GPS\"** message will be not be displayed until after flight begins, since EKF yaw alignment will not occur until sufficient ground speed is acquired for the GPS to provide a heading. This is normal in this situation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:134
#: f674f778f49e48f6ac2197dab83e7f1a
msgid "How do I reduce throttle oscillation in auto flight?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:136
#: 012c4bcb860d40e9bf30e7613f37b8f2
msgid "There are 3 parameters that affect the amount the throttle changes in automatic flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:139
#: 95d452f6a6d144fb80d40cf002343e64
msgid ":ref:`THR_SLEWRATE<THR_SLEWRATE>` is the percentage of throttle change allowed per second. A value of 100 means the throttle cannot change over its full range in less than 1 second."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:142
#: c89965177b0f4c6bb94c3a53bdff2175
msgid ":ref:`TECS_THR_DAMP<TECS_THR_DAMP>` is a damping factor for throttle control. The default is 0.5. A higher value will dampen throttle changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:144
#: 804bf33272c54035a7f6224706ffca8c
msgid ":ref:`TECS_TIME_CONST<TECS_TIME_CONST>` is the overall time constant for both throttle and pitch changes in TECS. It controls how rapidly TECS tries to correct for any error in speed or height. It is in seconds, and defaults to 5. A higher value makes the pitch and throttle corrections happen more slowly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:151
#: 18cfef6a83304bfdafe1e8a9eb5945e2
msgid "Why do I get small surface movements in ground tests?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:153
#: 0c8ef26bf31d4595ab50992eaec8b9e8
msgid "Before takeoff it is common to look at the amount of movement of ailerons and elevator when the plane is rolled and pitched on the ground. Some users have wondered why the amount of movement they see in this test is less in recent releases of the firmware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:158
#: dbf5a21daaf74c52862b32ce4ab47b25
msgid "The reason is the new :ref:`stall prevention code <stall-prevention>`. When the plane is on the ground the airspeed is very low, so is always under the minimum airspeed set in :ref:`AIRSPEED_MIN<AIRSPEED_MIN>`. That means the maximum roll demand is limited to 25 degrees, which means the amount of demanded aileron surface movement is less than it would be without stall prevention."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:164
#: c1f007f7c91c4b4b8a0c88552ee2e6cc
msgid "If you want to see what the movement would be without stall prevention then just set :ref:`STALL_PREVENTION<STALL_PREVENTION>` = 0. Remember to turn it back on before you fly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:171
#: 4ba67fe2b9ff44889428c130b1a52e0c
msgid "How would I setup crow flaps?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:173
#: a6f266ed7acc4897a7c4f50506072d5b
msgid "Crow flaps combine flaperons with normal flaps, but the flaperons move upward when the flaps are engaged. Crow flaps can add a lot of drag to slow an aircraft for landing without inducing a lot of pitching moment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:177
#: d4b41358653d4dc3bc71eabcd3408997
msgid "To setup crow flaps you :ref:`setup flaperons <flaperons-on-plane>` on two output channels just as you would for normal flaperons. However, follow the crow instructions to make sure that the ailerons move upward when the flap channel is activated, instead of downward."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:182
#: 27f30a8ab5f648eeacabbcb0560efbf3
msgid "Why do my servos jitter when on the ground?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:184
#: fb7c8b25c7354d40b0d4d5ee010dae56
msgid "When the aircraft is on the ground in a mode where it is doing attitude stabilization (such as FBWA mode) the servos often move about a small amount, even though the aircraft is not moving."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:188
#: c22092a0d5d24973a234d95ce6c569bb
msgid "The reason this happens is the attitude estimation code is doing its best to estimate the attitude of the aircraft, and it is getting a small amount of false input. The sources of the false input are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:192
#: 5582629ea0bf4db3bd760c308be5c4ad
msgid "if you have GPS lock then the GPS may be reporting a small amount of velocity change (GPS noise). This gets used to correct the accelerometers and comes out as a small amount of attitude noise, resulting in small attitude corrections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:196
#: 91ce2c66ac9c42f4aa4d6023c2f8eb70
msgid "if you don't have GPS lock but you have an airspeed sensor then the DCM code will try to use cross-product of the airspeed with the gyros to estimate inertial force corrections to the accelerometers. The airspeed is quite noisy at low speed, so this effect can be quite large"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:204
#: fff48b69a91449b395480e040635f60e
msgid "How is airspeed used with no airspeed sensor?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:206
#: cbe0fb6ce087483aac8d4dc288a46847
msgid "When you have an aircraft with no airspeed sensor Plane uses a range of techniques to fly as reliably as possible despite the lack of airspeed sensor data. The techniques are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:210
#: 76888ce2b742400b9060b85325746592
msgid "a synthetic airspeed estimate is calculated by the AHRS system by combining a wind estimate, the GPS ground speed and the response of the aircraft when turning. This airspeed estimate is usually quite good, although it is not as accurate as a real airspeed sensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:214
#: b18acbb48e1f42a4b4f48b1afa9ee367
msgid "for speed and height control, a different algorithm in TECS is used that does not rely on an airspeed measurement. The algorithm primarily relies on using throttle to maintain the desired height, relying on the fact that an aircraft will start to sink if its airspeed is too low. See the TECS code for full details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:219
#: 66f11facb72c443e9e7621de2fbf8629
msgid "For surface speed scaling (the change in control surface movement needed with different airspeed) the synthetic airspeed estimate is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:222
#: 70448f10881a4c41b1c67a293c695862
msgid "For stall prevention (if enabled) the synthetic airspeed is used"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:224
#: 49393c23c72b45749005b490b7daacec
msgid "When no airspeed sensor is available some parameters are not used for some purposes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:227
#: 3c2f3899f9194f58a0e0ffd8f3e1be11
msgid "the :ref:`AIRSPEED_CRUISE<AIRSPEED_CRUISE>` parameter is not used as an airspeed target in auto flight. Instead the :ref:`TRIM_THROTTLE<TRIM_THROTTLE>` parameter is used as base throttle, with extra throttle added/removed to retain the target altitude"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:231
#: 101ca4a7e42c4ae99786dcde251cb091
msgid "the :ref:`AIRSPEED_MIN<AIRSPEED_MIN>` and :ref:`AIRSPEED_MAX<AIRSPEED_MAX>` parameters are not used for airspeed limiting in TECS, but they are still used for the stall prevention code, using the synthetic airspeed value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:236
#: aac1efcca64a4023b5fa4d2f2a9d97ab
msgid "Why does my trim change when I change modes?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:238
#: 9e3277a525214e3f93a7bf130aa58590
msgid "Some people experience a problem where their roll or pitch trim changes when they change flight modes. So for example, in FBWA the plane is flying level, but when changed to MANUAL mode the plane is no longer in level trim."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:242
#: 0465bd3a0e0c4b458931f2a047fdb2fd
msgid "One cause of this is that you have a transmitter that has per flight mode trims. The Taranis is a good example of this if you use its built in flight mode controls. You need to be sure  your transmitter trim settings are the same, so that the stick inputs are the same in all flight modes and  match those you used for the RC calibration setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/fixed-wing-faq.rst:248
#: c10033b80ccf47ed9fbc30220df3b56e
msgid "Another, is that you are not using :ref:`SERVO_AUTO_TRIM<SERVO_AUTO_TRIM>` to automatically adjust the trim of the pitch and roll controlling flying surfaces. Enabling this will assure that MANUAL mode trim matches that of the auto-leveled modes, like STABILIZE and FBWA."
msgstr ""
